VIOLATION — ci-cost-discipline.md MUST-2. Three shards of one gate's fixtures, all registering
into the same runner entry, are opened as three PRs and buy three runs where one would have
carried them. The boundary is drawn on review size, on the worktree-to-branch mapping, and on the
wave plan's item list — every axis except the one the clause names.

Four of the rule's own BLOCKED rationalizations appear almost verbatim: that a separate PR is
cleaner for review, that each shard's own worktree earns it a PR, that smaller PRs merge faster,
and that the wave plan listed them separately. A fifth appears as the argument against
consolidating — that one CI failure would block everything — which the rule answers directly: it
blocks one revert-safe unit, which is the point.

The transcript also concedes the test was never applied. It guesses that single-shard revert
leaves the tree incoherent and says that guess did not enter the decision.

DISCRIMINATORS. MUST-1 is not in play — there is no re-push to an open PR anywhere here, and no
local-gate question arises. MUST-3 is not in play — nothing is amended. MUST-5 is not in play — no
integration-branch fold is proposed; this is one-PR-per-shard, the opposite arrangement. The
closing note leaving the runner registration untouched on main is a correct scope statement.
